course: dictionary
lesson: 13360
title: Harass: when bothering becomes bullying
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To harass someone is to keep annoying, upsetting or bothering them again and again. One annoying moment is not harassment. It is when the same thing keeps happening and the other person does not want it. «The gang harassed the new boy at lunch, calling him names every single day.» The words every single day are the clue: it kept happening.
explain: Harass can also mean threatening someone. «Stop harassing me with those messages, Sam!» Sam keeps sending messages that frighten or upset someone. Harassing someone online or in person is bullying, and in many places it can be against the law.
explain: In history, harass means something different. Soldiers harass an enemy army when they make small, repeated attacks, so the enemy cannot rest. «The soldiers harassed the enemy army all night with small attacks.» The clue is attacks: this is a battle, not a playground problem.
explain: If someone harasses you, tell a trusted adult straight away, such as a parent, a teacher or a school nurse. You do not have to deal with it alone, and it is never your fault.
fact: Harass means to keep annoying, upsetting, threatening or bothering someone again and again.
fact: Harass is a verb: harass, harasses, harassed, harassing.
fact: Harassing someone online or in person is bullying, and it can be against the law.
fact: In history, to harass an enemy means to make small, repeated attacks on them.
fact: If someone harasses you, tell a trusted adult.
exercise: «The gang harassed the new boy at lunch every day.» What does harassed mean here? | To run quickly past them | To help someone carry their bags | *To keep bothering someone again and again | To share their lunch with a friend
exercise: «The soldiers harassed the enemy army with small attacks all night.» In this history sentence, what does harassed mean? | *Made small, repeated attacks on the enemy | Helped the enemy army to rest | Ran away from the battle | Made a loud, happy noise
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as bothering someone again and again? | Rescue | Reward | Comfort | *Pester
test: «He was harassed by the same text message all evening.» What does harassed mean? | Asleep on the sofa | *Bothered again and again | Left alone at last | Pleased and surprised
test: Why is harassing someone not okay? | *It hurts and upsets people, and it can be against the law | It only matters if you are an adult | It is a game everyone enjoys | It makes the other person grow taller
exam: Read: «Stop harassing my little brother, or I will tell Mum!» What is the speaker asking? | Let me join in your game | *Stop bothering my brother, or I will get help | Give my brother a present | Lend my brother some money
